```java
// === DOMAIN (no framework imports) ===
public record Order(String id, String customerId, BigDecimal total) { }

// === DRIVING PORT (use case interface, owned by core) ===
public interface PlaceOrderUseCase {
    Order place(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items);
}

// === DRIVEN PORT (repository contract, owned by core) ===
public interface OrderRepository {
    Order save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findById(String id);
}

// === USE CASE (application service, in the core) ===
public class PlaceOrderService implements PlaceOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final InventoryGateway inventory;

    public PlaceOrderService(OrderRepository repo, InventoryGateway inv) {
        this.orderRepo = repo;
        this.inventory = inv;
    }

    @Override
    public Order place(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        inventory.reserve(items);
        Order order = Order.create(customerId, items);
        return orderRepo.save(order);
    }
}

// === DRIVING ADAPTER (HTTP, in adapters package) ===
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private final PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrder;
    @PostMapping("/orders")
    public OrderResponse create(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
        Order o = placeOrder.place(req.customerId(), req.items());
        return OrderResponse.from(o);
    }
}

// === DRIVEN ADAPTER (JPA, in adapters package) ===
@Repository
class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final OrderJpaEntityRepo jpa;
    @Override
    public Order save(Order order) {
        OrderEntity entity = OrderMapper.toEntity(order);
        return OrderMapper.toDomain(jpa.save(entity));
    }
}
```

Notice:
- `OrderRepository` is in the core, defined by the core's needs (not JPA-shaped).
- `JpaOrderRepository` is the adapter; converts between domain and JPA entities.
- Use case knows nothing of JPA, HTTP, Spring annotations.

Because the domain depends only on its own ports, testing in isolation is trivial:

```java
class PlaceOrderServiceTest {
    @Test
    void places_order_when_inventory_available() {
        OrderRepository fakeRepo = new InMemoryOrderRepository();
        InventoryGateway fakeInv = items -> { /* always succeeds */ };
        PlaceOrderService svc = new PlaceOrderService(fakeRepo, fakeInv);

        Order placed = svc.place("cust-1", List.of(item("sku-1", 2)));

        assertThat(placed.customerId()).isEqualTo("cust-1");
        assertThat(fakeRepo.findById(placed.id())).isPresent();
    }
}
```

- No Spring context (`@SpringBootTest` not needed).
- No DB / Testcontainers / H2.
- Pure JUnit + a couple of in-memory implementations.
- Tests run in milliseconds, not seconds.

Integration tests still exist for adapters (testing JPA repository against real Postgres), but the *use case logic* is tested in pure isolation.

**Layered version:**
```java
@Service
class OrderService {
    @Autowired OrderRepository orderRepo;  // Spring Data JPA interface

    @Transactional
    public Order placeOrder(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        OrderEntity entity = new OrderEntity(customerId, items);  // JPA entity
        return orderRepo.save(entity);                            // returns JPA entity
    }
}
```
Domain coupled to JPA: entity is `@Entity`, repository is `JpaRepository`.

**Hexagonal version:**
```java
// in core, no Spring, no JPA
public class PlaceOrderService implements PlaceOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;  // a port (plain interface)

    public PlaceOrderService(OrderRepository repo) { this.orderRepo = repo; }

    public Order place(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        Order order = Order.create(customerId, items);   // domain object
        return orderRepo.save(order);                    // domain object
    }
}

// in adapter, can be JPA or anything
@Repository
class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    public Order save(Order order) { ... convert + jpa.save ... }
}
```
Domain is plain Java; adapter handles the JPA conversion.
